Marvin Minsky writes:
>>>>By learning to think for themselves, of course, the first step in that
>>>>direction being to copy other people who are known to do it well.
>>>
>>>In college I attached
>>>myself to a great young mathematician, Andrew Gleason, and a great young
>>>psychologist, George Miller.  In graduate school I was attached to my
>>>fellow students John McCarthy and John Nash ...
>>>The trick is to hang around with your idols, try to anticipate how
>>>they'll solve each problem, or how they'll explain it. 
>>
>>Alas, this strategy isn't open to most of us.  We must learn to think
>>for ourselves mostly by ourselves.
>
>Umm, maybe not -- but I didn't mean to say that this began in college.  One
>needs at least one parent that is worth copying; I also had an excellent
>sister, five years older, to imitate.  ... their attachment-bonds are
>powerful influences on how children develop.   

I quite agree on the importance of personal relationships, on the
value of good idols, and the degree to which we can learn by imitating
others.  But I focused on the phrase "think for themselves", which I
associate with the step in maturity where one no longer just passes on
interesting ideas one heard from elsewhere, but actively contributes
good ideas of one's own.  This is hard to learn from someone's books
or lectures - since the process of how people evolve ideas is
usually hidden in such final products.  But I know you can learn this
by being there with someone good as they evolve ideas, as you did
with Nash, Miller, etc.

This happened to me somewhat in interacting with Eric Drexler and
company years ago.  But for most of my intellectual life I've felt
mostly on my own, and yet I think I did learn to think for myself.  I
say this not to brag, but to encourage those of you out there who feel
cut off from personal interactions with "big thinkers" to keep at it
-- you can mature and contribute even so.

Robin Hanson

