From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Sun, 23 Oct 1994 14:45:07 -0800
Subject: [#94-10-465] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

Robin Hanson writes (regarding purposeful code revisions by the uploaded):
>
>Consider most any huge complex computer program, compiled to assembly
>code.  This doesn't code for a sensitive growth process, but it is
>still pretty hard to improve it via low level modifications if one
>doesn't understand it at higher levels.  But I don't mind you trying,
>on yourself of course, when you're an upload - maybe you'll show me
>wrong.
>
To further your analogy consider what happens to code after years or
decades of maintaince. The original programmers are no longer around. No
one quite grasps the overall concept of how it works, or sometimes even
what it does. Programming tools have changed and the source looks like a
treatise on the history of programming styles. At some point the best way
to "maintain" the program is to start over with the vastly improved tools
of today. Plus this forces one to understand what the program really does.

Biological evolution seems to me like an extreem case of this
successive-patches approach. (Consider human elliminative plumbing). So
perhaps we  are ripe for a top-to-bottom rewrite by AI's smart enough to
grasp the systems-view of human neural wiring.

After such a cleanup we could try on fairly drastic mental alterations with
less chance of "crashing" or becoming something unpleasnt. Plus you could
keep a backup of your last brain handy.



