From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Sun, 23 Oct 1994 19:04:18 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-467] Brain backup proposal

>From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
>
>This is a discussion by Keith Lynch--kfl@access.digex.com. He's
>interested in comments on it, so please cc to him as well as to
>the list.
>
>*********************************************************************
>
>What's the easiest way to back up a living brain?

Your usage of the word "easiest" is certainly novel.

>
>Here's what I believe is the simplest method of brain backup.  It does
>require nanotechnology, but in a fairly simple form, much simpler than
>would be required for biostasis or for brain repair.
>
>I'm assuming that a person's personality, memories, aspirations, and
>identity (self-circuit, in Robert Ettinger's words) are completely (or
>at least, sufficently) defined by their neural connectivity diagram.
>This diagram would consist entirely of information on what the odds
>are of each neuron firing, given the current state (firing vs. not
>firing) of all the adjacent neurons, and given the current levels of
>each neurotransmitter chemical in the brain.  If this assumption is
>wrong, my backup method won't work.  (Does anyone have any good evidence
>for or against it?)

Of course the question is: "What are the important variables?".  If like
Kieth we want to make backup brains before we know the answer to this question,
then we probably should try to record everything that might potentially be
important.  If so, then it's not enough to know levels of neurotransmitters.
One must know, for example: concentrations of receptors for various
nt's and their distribution along the dendrites, soma, and axon terminals;
concentrations and distributions of any secondary messenger molecules
like G-proteins, calcium, cAMP, peptides, etc.; distribution of microtubules
and MAPs; length and width of all dendritic segments; length and width
of axon and degree of myelination; expression of genes at the cell body;
to name just some of the currently popular neural machinery.

One could of course assume that the important variable is simply the transfer
function at a synapse.  That is, we measure the spikes (frequency, width,
refractory period, amplitude) of the presynaptic neuron and compare
that with the potential change at the postsynaptic membrane.  This function
must depend on the history of both the presynaptic cell (transmitter
mobilization, receptor density, various secondary messengers...) and
the postsynaptic cell (current depolarization, Ca++, receptor activation
and density...).  It will also depend on nearby neurons that might make
pre- or post-synaptic contact near this synapse.  Now we've got the
context-dependent transfer function for each synapse of a neuron.  Next
we need to store the dynamics of action potential generation at the cell body -
this should consist at least of a correlation between the spatio-temporal
pattern of depolarizations at various incoming synapses.  Such information
might capture implicitly details of cell geometry that we'd like to
avoid storing.  But this is still just storing stuff for one cell...

>
>The subject would be injected with about 10^12 nanoprobes.  Each probe
>would circulate in the bloodstream until it found itself touching a
>neuron.  Then, it would attach itself to the neuron.  It would emit a
>chemical signal whenever the neuron fires.  That's all it does.

Presumably the probe would first have to navigate through the blood-brain
barrier.  Finding neurons might not be so hard - one might search for the Tau
molecule which seems to be expressed only in axons (although a double-check
might be good).  Also, making a mistake like latching onto a glial cell might
not be so bad, especially if we use 10^12 probes...

>
>It doesn't measure or report the neuron's location, or the particular
>geometry or chemistry of the neuron or its synapses, as that information
>is assumed to be important only to the extent that it influences what
>the probe *is* measuring and reporting.

>
>A tiny proportion of these probes would emit a chemical signal which
>reports the current levels of various neurotransmitters.  (I'm assuming
>that the level of every neurotransmitter is roughly the same across the
>whole brain at any given time.  Does anyone know if this is true?)

Not remotely true.

>
>The chemical signal consists of two binary numbers.  The first is the
>ID number of the probe.  Each probe has a unique 40 bit number which
>never changes.  The other is a timestamp.  The probe contains a clock
>which counts milliseconds since the epoch.  The epoch is the same for
>all the probes, and is just before they were injected.  (I'm assuming
>a one millisecond time resolution is sufficient, given that neural
>firing frequencies are typically on the order of 10 Hz.  If it's not
>sufficient, this proposal can be reworked with the higher frequency.)

Actually, firing ranges up to 200Hz.  Some maintain that finer structure of
the firing also is important, though it's unclear how such high freq's would
not be lost in the noise.  So yes, increase with higher frequency, and don't
forget to store spike widths and refractory periods.

>
>How would the clocks be kept synchronized?  I think it would be possible
>for each probe to pick up the shortwave time stations WWV and CHU,
>using the inside of the neuron as an antenna and the outside as a
>counterpoise.  The signal wouldn't be strong, but it should be stronger
>than anything else at those frequencies.  A probe wouldn't have to be
>able to pick up these stations continuously, just often enough that its
>clock doesn't drift more than a millisecond.  If any probe's clock does
>drift too far, its data won't correlate with anything else, and will be
>discarded in the analysis phase, unless it can be gotten back into sync
>by trying various fudge factors.

If the shortwave is strong enough such that the neuron acts as an antenna, then
it's likely to screw up the neuron's activity.  With coupled oscillators, any
entrainment signal is likely to be significant, so you'd probably screw any
pattern generators in the brain.

>
>There is considerable redundancy in the system.  There are about 10^11
>neurons in the brain, thus the average neuron will have ten probes.

I assume that some probes will be bigger than others.  It would hardly be wise
to use the same device to record a spinal interneuron (~2 synapses) as to
record a Purkinje cell (~100,000 synapses).

>The data will be temporarily segregated by probe number.  For each
>pair of probes -- all 5*10^23 ways to select two of 10^12 -- the
>all-over correlation coefficient will be looked at.

10^23 is a pretty big number.  I think losing all the connectivity information
and appealing to correlation to reconstruct it is a big mistake.  Let's
consider what would be necessary to reconstruct the connectivity:  For
correlation to be at all informative, you'd like to get at least one neural
firing per potential connection to be able to distinguish correlations due to a
connection from those due to chance.  That is, you'd need about 10^11 firings.
Given an "average" firing of an "average" neuron of 50Hz, this will
take about 60 years.  And I would advise against inducing seisures to
reduce this to say, 10 years...

... I'll pass on commenting on the rest of the proposal ...

>I eagerly await comments and criticism.
>-- 
>Keith Lynch, kfl@access.digex.com
>
>f p=2,3:2 s q=1 x "f f=3:2 q:f*f>p!'q  s q=p#f" w:q p,?$x\8+1*8
>

Since you asked, I will offer my comments.  Hold off on getting a patent...

The endeavour is certainly an admirable one; a brain backup would be very
nice.  But in your discussion you've completely ommitted what is the central
issue - the neural variables that have to be stored.  Who cares whether the
information is stored in fatty acids or aliphatic hydrocarbons if this
information is meaningless?  As is common in proposals of this type, you focus
on the issues where there is knowledge - storage organization, correlation
methods (which would be pretty tough if you've lost the connectivity info),
ASCII codes, wiring diagrams - and ignore the issues where we know little.
Unfortunately, these dark ugly issues are completely central to the project.
It seems your discussion hopes to instill in the reader (an I suspect in the
writer as well) a feeling of hope that the project is within our technological
reach, by concentrating on the surface of the issue and shying away from the
murky depths.  Well, no amount of wishful thinking and blissful ignorance will
make them any less murky.  So let's not fool ourselves by proposing
solutions that might sound good and comfort us with lots of numbers.
Instead let's work on what the real issue is - an understanding of brain
function.  And the best place to look for that understanding is not in brute
force engineering, but by studying the brain (imagine that)...

Paul


